1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,905
A shiny, waxy leaf.

2
00:00:01,905 --> 00:00:03,757
Soft hairs on a young stem.

3
00:00:03,757 --> 00:00:06,724
Thin green veins you can
see against the light.

4
00:00:07,124 --> 00:00:09,326
Three things you can see from outside.

5
00:00:09,326 --> 00:00:12,454
Each one belongs to a different
system inside the plant.

6
00:00:12,904 --> 00:00:17,480
Diya held a leaf up to the window and
asked: is a plant built in layers,

7
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:18,507
like a house?

8
00:00:18,857 --> 00:00:19,757
Yes.

9
00:00:19,329 --> 00:00:23,705
Every plant organ is built from three
tissue systems: a skin, a filling,

10
00:00:23,705 --> 00:00:24,605
and pipes.

11
00:00:25,010 --> 00:00:29,810
By the end, you will name all three,
open a stoma up close, and sort the

12
00:00:29,810 --> 00:00:31,863
four kinds of vascular bundles.

13
00:00:32,513 --> 00:00:35,056
First, the three systems, from outside in.

14
00:00:35,406 --> 00:00:37,985
The epidermal tissue system is the skin.

15
00:00:37,985 --> 00:00:39,772
It covers the whole plant.

16
00:00:40,222 --> 00:00:44,557
The ground tissue system is the
filling: everything except the skin and

17
00:00:44,557 --> 00:00:46,009
the vascular bundles.

18
00:00:46,459 --> 00:00:51,363
The vascular tissue system is the
pipes: xylem and phloem, packed together

19
00:00:51,363 --> 00:00:52,864
in vascular bundles.

20
00:00:53,514 --> 00:00:55,613
Let's zoom into the skin of a leaf.

21
00:00:55,963 --> 00:00:59,061
The epidermis is usually
one layer of cells.

22
00:00:59,061 --> 00:01:03,364
Each cell has a big vacuole,
and a thin lining of cytoplasm.

23
00:01:03,864 --> 00:01:08,900
On the outside sits the cuticle, a
thick waxy layer that stops water loss.

24
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:10,331
That is the shine.

25
00:01:10,331 --> 00:01:11,969
Roots have no cuticle.

26
00:01:12,469 --> 00:01:13,369
Zoom closer.

27
00:01:13,293 --> 00:01:15,178
Here is a stoma, a tiny pore.

28
00:01:15,178 --> 00:01:17,352
Two bean-shaped guard cells surround it.

29
00:01:17,352 --> 00:01:20,579
They have chloroplasts, and
they open and close the pore.

30
00:01:21,079 --> 00:01:24,214
Some cells beside them
become subsidiary cells.

31
00:01:24,214 --> 00:01:29,175
The pore, the guard cells and the
subsidiary cells together make the stomatal

32
00:01:29,175 --> 00:01:30,075
apparatus.

33
00:01:30,516 --> 00:01:33,302
In grasses, the guard
cells are dumb-bell shaped.

34
00:01:33,302 --> 00:01:37,383
And on the stem, the skin grows hairs
called trichomes, which help prevent

35
00:01:37,383 --> 00:01:38,283
water loss.

36
00:01:38,827 --> 00:01:39,887
Now the pipes.

37
00:01:39,887 --> 00:01:42,694
A vascular bundle can
be built in four ways.

38
00:01:43,044 --> 00:01:43,944
Radial.

39
00:01:43,678 --> 00:01:48,158
Xylem and phloem sit on different
radii, taking turns around the centre.

40
00:01:48,158 --> 00:01:49,933
Roots are built like this.

41
00:01:50,683 --> 00:01:51,583
Conjoint.

42
00:01:51,441 --> 00:01:55,963
Xylem and phloem sit together on the
same radius, with phloem usually on

43
00:01:55,963 --> 00:01:57,141
the outer side.

44
00:01:57,141 --> 00:01:59,657
Stems and leaves are built like this.

45
00:02:00,407 --> 00:02:01,307
Open.

46
00:02:00,982 --> 00:02:05,625
In dicot stems, a strip called
cambium lies between phloem and xylem.

47
00:02:05,625 --> 00:02:08,634
Cambium can make new
xylem and phloem later.

48
00:02:09,384 --> 00:02:10,284
Closed.

49
00:02:09,924 --> 00:02:11,856
In monocots, there is no cambium.

50
00:02:11,856 --> 00:02:14,426
So the bundle cannot make
new xylem and phloem.

51
00:02:15,176 --> 00:02:17,506
And the filling around the bundles?

52
00:02:17,506 --> 00:02:22,068
In stems and roots, the ground tissue
forms the cortex, pericycle, pith

53
00:02:22,068 --> 00:02:23,430
and medullary rays.

54
00:02:23,430 --> 00:02:25,661
In leaves, it is the mesophyll.

55
00:02:26,411 --> 00:02:30,437
Now the hardest pair to tell
apart: open and closed bundles.

56
00:02:30,787 --> 00:02:32,060
Both are conjoint.

57
00:02:32,060 --> 00:02:33,879
Both have xylem and phloem.

58
00:02:33,879 --> 00:02:35,650
Both carry water and food.

59
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:40,087
But an open bundle has cambium
between its phloem and xylem.

60
00:02:40,087 --> 00:02:41,923
A closed bundle has none.

61
00:02:42,573 --> 00:02:43,473
Your turn.

62
00:02:43,403 --> 00:02:45,713
A maize stem bundle has no cambium.

63
00:02:45,713 --> 00:02:47,383
Is it open, or closed?

64
00:02:47,383 --> 00:02:48,604
Pause and decide.

65
00:02:51,804 --> 00:02:52,704
Closed!

66
00:02:52,445 --> 00:02:53,841
Maize is a monocot.

67
00:02:53,841 --> 00:02:57,779
With no cambium, its bundle
cannot make new xylem and phloem.

68
00:02:58,429 --> 00:02:59,407
Remember this.

69
00:02:59,407 --> 00:03:02,906
Skin, filling and pipes:
epidermal, ground and vascular.

70
00:03:03,506 --> 00:03:05,041
Now, a worked example.

71
00:03:05,041 --> 00:03:07,656
Let's sort four parts
into the right system.

72
00:03:08,006 --> 00:03:09,895
Guard cells around a pore.

73
00:03:09,895 --> 00:03:11,937
That is the epidermal system.

74
00:03:12,387 --> 00:03:14,167
Mesophyll inside a leaf.

75
00:03:14,167 --> 00:03:16,116
That is the ground system.

76
00:03:16,566 --> 00:03:19,010
Xylem and phloem packed in a bundle.

77
00:03:19,010 --> 00:03:20,939
That is the vascular system.

78
00:03:21,389 --> 00:03:23,674
Pith and medullary rays in a stem.

79
00:03:23,674 --> 00:03:25,805
That is the ground system again.

80
00:03:26,455 --> 00:03:27,917
Now, the exam trap.

81
00:03:28,267 --> 00:03:33,068
Many students think every epidermis
has a waxy cuticle, even the root.

82
00:03:33,468 --> 00:03:35,803
But the cuticle is absent in roots.

83
00:03:35,803 --> 00:03:38,795
A root must take water
in, not keep it out.

84
00:03:39,445 --> 00:03:40,958
Where is this asked?

85
00:03:40,958 --> 00:03:45,647
As statements: guard cell shapes,
where the cuticle is absent, and open

86
00:03:45,647 --> 00:03:47,200
versus closed bundles.

87
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:48,932
Exam level now.

88
00:03:48,932 --> 00:03:51,147
Read the assertion and the reason.

89
00:03:51,147 --> 00:03:52,327
Pause and solve.

90
00:03:57,727 --> 00:03:58,790
The answer is A.

91
00:03:58,790 --> 00:04:01,707
Both are true, and the reason
explains the assertion.

92
00:04:02,207 --> 00:04:06,343
Monocot bundles have no cambium
between their xylem and phloem.

93
00:04:06,843 --> 00:04:11,305
Without cambium, they cannot form new
xylem and phloem, so they are called

94
00:04:11,305 --> 00:04:12,205
closed.

95
00:04:12,527 --> 00:04:14,067
Here is your recap.

96
00:04:14,067 --> 00:04:15,650
Screenshot this card.

97
00:04:18,850 --> 00:04:21,036
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

98
00:04:21,436 --> 00:04:22,336
One.

99
00:04:21,888 --> 00:04:23,703
Name the three tissue systems.

100
00:04:24,603 --> 00:04:25,503
Two.

101
00:04:25,042 --> 00:04:27,481
What shape are the guard cells in grasses?

102
00:04:28,381 --> 00:04:29,281
Three.

103
00:04:28,932 --> 00:04:31,288
Where are radial vascular bundles found?

104
00:04:32,388 --> 00:04:34,749
Next time: slice a root across.

105
00:04:34,749 --> 00:04:36,630
What hides at its centre?

106
00:04:36,630 --> 00:04:40,130
We find out in anatomy of
dicot and monocot roots.
